  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 025: EUV availability converts installed scanners into productive capacity, so uptime and service can matter almost as much as tool count.

Lesson 026: High-NA EUV improves resolution by raising numerical aperture, but its value depends on whether it removes more manufacturing complexity than it introduces.

Lesson 027: EUV pellicles protect reusable reticles from repeating contamination defects, but their transmission and thermal behavior can affect scanner throughput.

Lesson 028: An EUV mask is a patterned multilayer mirror, so reflectivity, absorber geometry and buried defects all influence what is actually printed on the wafer.

Lesson 029: Mask inspection and repair separate harmless imperfections from printable defects before one master-pattern error can repeat across many exposures.

The big idea

Before a scanner can project a circuit pattern onto a wafer, someone has to create the master pattern on the reticle itself.

That is the job of the **mask writer**.

A mask writer takes digital chip-layout data and uses an electron beam to expose a resist-coated mask blank. The mask is then developed and etched so its absorber pattern becomes the master image used by lithography.

The economic problem is that advanced masks have become so geometrically complicated that writing them one feature at a time can take an impractical amount of time.

The industry’s answer is **multi-beam electron-beam writing**: instead of drawing with one beam, write with roughly a quarter-million beamlets in parallel.

This is a useful bottleneck lesson because the mask writer normally sits upstream of the wafer fab. It does not process every production wafer.

But if mask writing is too slow, a new chip design cannot get into the fab quickly enough.

## 1. How a mask is physically written

Start with the finished digital layout for one lithography layer.

Software first converts that layout into detailed exposure instructions.

Then the physical flow is roughly:

mask blank → e-beam resist → electron-beam exposure → develop → absorber etch → clean → inspect → repair/qualify

The writer performs the exposure step.

Why electrons?

An electron beam can be focused extremely tightly and steered electronically. Unlike optical lithography, the writer does not need another physical mask above it.

It is **direct write**: digital data controls where electrons hit the resist.

That makes e-beam writing precise enough to manufacture the master pattern that an optical or EUV scanner will later reproduce many times.

## 2. The traditional approach: variable-shaped beam

For decades, advanced mask writers commonly used **variable-shaped beam**, or VSB, technology.

A VSB system shapes one electron beam into rectangles or other simple geometric shots.

Imagine drawing with a stamp whose width and height can change.

For simple rectangular patterns, this is efficient because one shot can cover a useful area.

But modern masks are not simple.

Optical proximity correction adds assist features and edge changes to compensate for how lithography distorts patterns. Inverse lithography can create even more complicated, sometimes curvilinear shapes.

The writer must break those shapes into a huge number of shots.

So VSB write time tends to rise with **shot count and pattern complexity**. At leading-edge complexity, industry discussions have described dense-mask write times stretching into tens of hours.

That becomes painful because inspection, repair and qualification cannot begin until writing is finished.

## 3. Multi-beam changes the scaling law

A multi-beam writer attacks the problem with parallelism.

